9.13.m:
Prajāpati is the Daśahotṛ;
the new- and full-moon sacrifices are the Caturhotṛ;
the Cāturmāsyas are the Pañcahotṛ;
the Soma sacrifice is the Saptahotṛ.
The Agnihotra is the Daśahotṛ;
the new- and full-moon sacrifices are the Caturhotṛ;
the animal sacrifice is the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ;
the Soma sacrifice is the Saptahotṛ.
The breath is the Daśahotṛ;
the eye is the Caturhotṛ;
the ear is the Pañcahotṛ;
speech and the self are the Saptahotṛ.
The Agnihotra is offered for long life; he attains full life who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for the eye; [it is] not for the seizure of his eye who knows thus.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for the ear; [it is] not for the seizure of his ear who knows thus.
The Soma sacrifice is offered for speech and for the self; [it is] not for the seizure of his speech nor of his self who knows thus.
Prajāpati is the Daśahotṛ.
Being one, he has the greatest share of the utterances, and being one he becomes many, who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for fiery splendor.
A Brahmin, indeed, belongs to Agni;
Agni is connected with the gāyatrī, has the gāyatrī metre;
he has made his own two deities far-extended;
no one else has power over his acquisition and preservation, who knows thus.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for long life;
he attains full life who knows thus.
The Soma sacrifice is offered for the heavenly world;
by it he goes to the heavenly world.

10.6.m:
The Naimiṣyas sat at a sacrificial session.
Rising up, they begged twenty-seven heifers among the Kuru-Pañcālas.
Vaka Dālbhi said to them:
"Divide these among yourselves;
I will go to this Dhṛtarāṣṭra Vaicitravīrya;
he will make a house for me."
He went to him;
he did not heed him;
he drove him away:
"These cows, O wretched Brahmin!"
He said:
"Paśupati kills cows;
cooking them further off, roam about!"
[thinking:] "For them, may the god-impelled one pour out food for the king for me!"
he cooked them, cutting out the thighs.
While he was cooking, [the kingdom] dried up.
He offered a cake of black rice on eight potsherds to Agni accompanied by Rudra.
Whatever belonged to Dhṛtarāṣṭra dawned upon as scattered and fleeing.
The diviners found out:
"This Brahmin is practicing sorcery against you;
take refuge with him!"
He sought to win him over;
he gave him much.
He offered a cake of white rice on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
Then indeed it dried away.
When practicing sorcery, he should offer a cake of black rice on eight potsherds to Agni accompanied by Rudra.
Agni is Rudra;
he delivers him over to Rudra.
It is of black rice;
black indeed is darkness;
darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat when this god assails creatures, when he desires: "May it dry away!"
This is Agni's healing body, the fragrant one;
he runs up to it with its share;
with it he heals them.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat about whom they hear that he has died when he has not died.
Foul-smelling indeed is he heard of who is heard of as having died;
that very body of Agni which is fragrant, to that he runs up with its share;
with it he makes him fragrant.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat whom they accuse without having slain.
Seized by defilement indeed is he whom they accuse without having slain;
that very body of Agni which is fragrant, to that he runs up with its share;
with it he purifies him.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni the food-eater who desires: "May I be an eater of food."
Agni is the food-eater among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him an eater of food.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni possessed of food who desires: "May I be possessed of food."
Agni is possessed of food among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him possessed of food.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni the lord of food who desires: "May I be the lord of food."
Agni is the lord of food among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him the lord of food.
He should offer it all around for a year;
the year is the giver of proper food;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him an eater of food, possessed of food, and a lord of food.
A Brahmin should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Vasumat for all desires.
Whatever one finds, that is wealth;
Agni is possessed of wealth among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he grants him all desires.

11.4.m:
One who is born after should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati made of after-ripening rice.
Bṛhaspati, indeed, was born after among the gods;
he came round to the foremost place.
Bṛhaspati is the deity of him who is born after;
he takes hold of him;
he leads him round to the foremost place.
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
the brahman is the father of the Brahmin;
the father has power over the son, whether forwards or backwards, not vice versa;
the father, the brahman itself, leads him round to the foremost place.
After-ripening, indeed, are these rice grains;
he who is born after—them he now leads round to the foremost place.
In that he prepares the offering,
he takes hold of them;
they lead him round to the foremost place.
The sacrificial grass is regrown; the kindling wood is regrown,
for success.
One who desires relatives should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati;
its accompanying and offering verses should contain [the word] 'group'.
A Brahmin, indeed, belongs to Bṛhaspati by deity;
he provides his own deity with an abundance.
The accompanying and offering verses contain [the word] 'group';
he makes him possessed of a group through his relatives.
One should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati;
its accompanying and offering verses should pertain to the Maruts, if one should desire: 'May there be the power of the brahman.'
Brahmaṇaspati is the brahman;
the Maruts are the commonalty;
he subjects the commonalty entirely to the brahman;
suddenly there is the power of the brahman.
One should offer a caru to Aryaman who desires: 'May my subjects be desirous of giving.'
He, indeed, is Aryaman who gives;
upon giving these subjects subsist;
they become desirous of giving to him.
One should offer a caru to Indra and Bṛhaspati for a nobleman who desires to thrive.
The gods, indeed, were afraid of the nobleman being born;
they pursued him and bound him with a cord.
Bound, indeed, is this nobleman born;
if he were born unbound, nothing would remain;
he would eat all these subjects completely down.
The nobleman belongs to Indra by deity;
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
by the brahman he releases him from the binding;
he, unbound, spreads out for manly vigor.
Twice-twisted gold is the sacrificial fee,
for cutting off that cord.
One who desires long life should offer a caru of a hundred kṛṣṇala weights in ghee to Prajāpati.
The gods, indeed, feared death;
they sought refuge with Prajāpati;
he sacrificed for them with this offering.
Gold is immortality;
with immortality he placed immortality in them;
thence, indeed, they became immortal.
He who fears death should sacrifice with this [offering].
Gold is immortality;
with immortality he places immortality in him.
It consists of a hundred kṛṣṇalas;
man has a hundred [years of] life, a hundred manly vigors;
he wins life and manly vigor.
It is to be performed to the Viśve Devas,
for he then sacrificed for all the gods;
it is to be performed to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the womb;
he firmly establishes himself in the womb.
Four kṛṣṇalas each form a portion,
for success.
They carry it around to the Brahman priest;
the Brahman priest is the brahman;
by the brahman he places life in him in one continuous stream.
He who desires, 'May I become wealthier,' should sacrifice with this.
This, indeed, is man's heavenly world, that he becomes wealthier in this world;
he thrives and becomes wealthier who, knowing thus, sacrifices with this.
One who has reached prosperity should offer a caru to Sūrya;
its cover should be a golden ornament,
with a silver one below.
Yonder sun is the end; having reached the end of prosperity, a man turns back;
from the end he grasps the end;
he does not become worse than he is when sacrificing.
Yonder sun was this [world];
he roamed burning this [world];
the gods, seizing him with these two, made him go to yonder world;
he did not shine;
they offered five golden kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings;
gold is brilliance;
with brilliance they placed brilliance in him;
thence he grasped brilliance;
he became brilliant along these five directions.
This [earth] is silver; yonder [sky] is golden;
seizing him with these two, he makes him go to yonder world.
They say: 'He did not shine,' for indeed he did not shine.
He should offer five golden kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings;
gold is brilliance;
with brilliance he unites him;
he becomes brilliant along these five directions.

11.5.m:
One who desires spiritual lustre should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra made of white rice grains; there should be churned ghee from a white cow with a white calf in it.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness.
He did not shine out.
For him the gods sought an expiation.
They sacrificed for him with this offering.
With it they struck away the darkness from him.
Covered by darkness is he who, being fit for spiritual lustre, does not possess spiritual lustre.
He strikes away darkness from him;
he unites him with spiritual lustre.
The ghee is from a white cow with a white calf, the rice grains are white.
Like this indeed is spiritual lustre;
he unites [him] with spiritual lustre.
They sprinkle with ghee;
they wipe off with ghee,
for non-appeasement;
for water is appeasement.
They should not even carry water into his house that night.
He should sacrifice in an enclosed space,
for enclosing spiritual lustre.
The caru is a span in size upwards, and so across.
So great indeed is man;
as far as his vital breaths extend, as great as his self is, from that he strikes away darkness.
They proceed together with the rays [of the sun];
that sun, rising, strikes away darkness from him.
He should sacrifice at the full moon of Tiṣya.
Tiṣya indeed is Rudra, the moon is Soma.
Belonging to Soma are the plants towards the east, belonging to Rudra towards the west.
They wither towards the east;
they wither towards the west.
Visibly he makes them prosper.
He who sacrifices with this is liable to become skin-diseased;
for he strikes away excessively from him.
He should also recite Manu's verses among the kindling verses.
Whatever Manu said was medicine;
he makes medicine for him.
'The refreshing rivers flow near'—with this Nārāśaṃsa verse he encloses,
for appeasement.
It has six feet;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
However, in that case he is certainly liable to become skin-diseased;
for ascending, he goes against [excessive] fiery energies.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru to Soma and Pūṣan as a subsequent offering.
Soma indeed is the Brahmin's own deity;
Pūṣan is cattle;
he strengthens his own deity with cattle;
he provides a skin [for himself].
One who practices sorcery should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra made of black rice grains.
Soma indeed is the lord of these creatures; Rudra is Agni; the lord rules over what is to be killed and what is not to be killed.
Having begged him off from the lord, he hands him over to Rudra.
It is of black rice grains;
black indeed is darkness, darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death itself.
The sacrificial grass is made of reed grass,
for breaking down.
The kindling wood is of vibhītaka,
for splitting apart.
One should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra for rivalry or for [obtaining] a second [partner].
Half of everything should belong to one and half to the other: half should be of white rice grains, half of black; half of the sacrificial grass should be of reed grass, half of darbha; half of the kindling wood should be of vibhītaka, half of another tree.
Soma indeed is the Brahmin's own deity;
he generates a second [partner] for him in his own deity.
That it is half and half,
that is for duality.
One should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra in milk for one who is diseased.
Belonging to Soma is the living, belonging to Agni the dead.
Neither living nor dead is he who is diseased;
from both of them he ransoms him by their share.
It is in milk;
milk indeed is milk;
man is milk;
milk is diseased in him who is diseased;
with milk he wins milk for him.
He who is the Hotṛ indeed drags him out swallowed by Soma and Rudra;
he is liable to die.
He should give an ox to him;
he should kill it;
he should eat of it;
that indeed is the expiation there.
One desiring offspring or desiring cattle should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra.
Soma indeed is the layer of semen;
Rudra is Agni, he is the procreator;
Soma places semen in him;
Agni procreates.
He obtains offspring or cattle, for whichever desire he offers.

11.8.m:
One who is going to sacrifice with this [rite] should offer on the previous day an eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni and Viṣṇu, a caru to Sarasvatī, and a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Agni indeed is all the deities; Viṣṇu is the sacrifice; Sarasvatī is speech; Bṛhaspati is the brahman.
These very deities he takes hold of on the previous day.
The vital breaths indeed are the deities;
having taken hold of the vital breaths, he fasts.
A sick man should offer tomorrow an eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni, a caru to Soma, a caru to Aditi, a caru made of barley to Varuṇa, and a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara.
To Agni indeed go the flesh-parts of a deceased man;
to Soma, the sap;
to the earth, the body.
This [earth] is Aditi;
for in her he lies.
These deities indeed have power over a man;
from them he rouses him upward.
Belonging to Soma is he while alive; belonging to Agni when deceased.
Neither alive nor dead is he who is sick;
from both of them he ransoms him with their share.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who is sick.
In that there is one to Varuṇa,
he frees him from Varuṇa.
It is made of barley.
It is a span in size.
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year; life is the year;
he establishes him in the year, in life.
He attains a complete lifespan.
These offerings are five.
Man is fivefold;
he secures the man.
'You are Agni's life,' [saying this] he places gold in the ghee.
The gold is born of Agni; mortals make life for Agni;
with them he bestows life on him.
'Indra's breath,' [he says].
Having these deities indeed are the breaths;
these he places in him.
Having these deities are the directions;
according to their deity he rouses him up from the directions.
He makes five oblations of these.
Man is fivefold;
he wins the man.
All the officiating priests speak around him;
all the officiating priests treat him.
They speak along the hand of the Brahman priest.
The Brahman is the brahman;
by the brahman alone they place life in him at once.
'Because you slithered,' [he says];
thereby he explains the birth, connection, and power of ghee.
'With the purifying stoma, you,' [he says].
Only so much exists as cups, stomas, and metres;
with as much as exists, with so much he treats him.
'I raise up, I release, let him lift up,' [he says].
Just as one raises up, releases, and lifts up one condemned to death, even so does he now raise him up, release him, and lift him up.
'The long-lived gods,' [saying this] he hands it to him.
These gods indeed are long-lived;
through them he bestows life on him.
He drinks it.
He drinks life itself.
Whatever of that ghee he would, as it were, suck out from the gold—
gold is brilliance; ghee is life;
from brilliance he places life in himself.
'Make him, O Agni, for life, for lustre,' [saying this] he ties on the gold.
Agni indeed is all deities;
gold is life;
with all deities he binds it on for him.
'Sharpen, O Varuṇa, sharp might,' [he says];
he whets him.
'Bestow protection on him, O Aditi, like a mother,' [he says].
This [earth] is Aditi;
he establishes him upon her.
'So that all the gods may let him reach old age,' [he says];
he makes him reach death from old age.
'The breath of the Aśvins, of Mitra and Varuṇa, of Bṛhaspati,' [he says].
Having these deities indeed are the breaths;
these he places in him;
these he arranges in him;
these he makes not depart from him.
